# --- the weekly run path: whoever owns the path writes what it refuses
#
# These files execute the Friday publish from the operator's checkout, and the run has one
# property nothing else here has: it cannot be retried inside its own window. A refusal that
# arrives late arrives after the solve is spent.
#
#   src/squadopt/platform/weekly_operations.py
#   src/squadopt/platform/weekly_publish.py
#   src/squadopt/application/weekly_plan.py
#
# Ownership below is unchanged by this rule and deliberately so: a change here needs the
# approvals its lines ask for and nothing extra (until 2026-09-25 that was all three roles),
# and the point is not to keep anyone out. Crossing into these files is often exactly right.
# Issue #551 asked SpeedyV5 to teach the runner a new flag and that was the correct call.
#
# The rule is about what must travel WITH the crossing. **A change that adds an input to the
# run path lands with the refusal that input needs, and the owner of the path writes that
# refusal**, because the refusal conventions live in the path and not in the feature.
#
# The case that produced this rule: --rotation-capture arrived correctly and without a shape
# check on its value, which the runner joined onto the snapshot root and handed to the journal
# to walk and digest. '../../..' resolved outside the repository and an empty string resolved
# to the snapshot root, so a mistyped identifier did not fail: it succeeded at reading the
# wrong thing. The flag was right, the refusal was missing, and the refusal was one function
# in the file whose owner knows that every other input there already has one.
#
# CODEOWNERS cannot enforce this; it decides approvals, not contents. It is written here
# because this is the file a reviewer of these paths already opens.

